// The question-first format (DECISIONS.md D21.1, D25.1): the question, a plain
// answer, the figure, then the code in Source notes. Links to the code live in
// the notes only.
const lang = "en";
const r = room("arc");
const A = "module/zfs/arc.c";
const L = {
  mintime: gh(A, 1318),
  anon: gh(A, 5381, 5397),
  mfuHit: gh(A, 5450, 5459),
  wt: gh(A, 4499, 4501),
  evictImpl: gh(A, 4386, 4398),
  evictedState: gh(A, 3828, 3830),
  destroy: gh(A, 3884, 3889),
  ghostDestroy: gh(A, 3820, 3823),
  mfuTrim: gh(A, 4579, 4582),
  hdrSize: gh(A, 1795, 1807),
  toMfu: gh(A, 5442, 5449),
  mfuGhost: gh(A, 5466, 5472),
  hits: gh(A, 4479, 4491),
  balance: gh(A, 459),
  fractions: gh("include/sys/arc_impl.h", 1032, 1035),
  init: gh(A, 8038, 8040),
  adjRest: gh(A, 4424, 4441),
  noEvictPath: gh(A, 8261, 8264),
  feed: gh(A, 9771),
  feedHz: gh(A, 9791),
  feedTail: gh(A, 9530, 9538),
  l2cOnly: gh(A, 3805, 3819),
  l2cHit: gh(A, 5481, 5488),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="arc"
  title="Why doesn’t a big read empty my cache? The ARC · Uberblok"
  description="ZFS keeps blocks read once apart from blocks read again, and remembers the blocks it pushed out, so one large scan cannot empty its cache. Interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)} · {r.title.en}</p>
      <h1>Why doesn’t a big read empty my cache?</h1>
      <div class="prose answer">
        <p class="lead">
          ZFS keeps the blocks it has read in memory, in a cache called the ARC, so that the next read of the same block
          does not go to the disk. When the cache is full, it has to choose which block to drop. A simple cache drops the
          block used longest ago. With that rule, one large read, such as a backup or a scan of a big file, fills the whole
          cache with blocks that will not be read again and pushes out the blocks your programs use all the time.
        </p>
        <p>
          The ARC keeps two lists instead of one. A block read once goes on the first list, called MRU (most recently
          used). A block read again later moves to the second list, MFU (most frequently used). Each list has a target
          share of the cache. When the cache is full, MRU gives up its oldest block if it is over its share, and MFU gives
          up its oldest block otherwise. A scan only adds blocks read once, so they go into MRU and push each other out,
          while the blocks in MFU stay.
        </p>
        <p>
          The shares are not fixed. When a block is pushed out, the ARC frees its data but keeps a small record of it, its
          header, on a ghost list. There is one ghost list for MRU and one for MFU. A read of a block that is still on a
          ghost list goes to the disk, but it shows that a larger list would have kept the block, so the ARC moves a little
          of the cache towards that list. A workload that keeps coming back to recent blocks makes MRU grow, and one that
          returns to older, often-read blocks makes MFU grow.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="lab">
      <h2 id="lab"><span class="n">1</span> Read, scan, read again</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The cache below holds 16 blocks. It starts with a hot set, H1 to H6, read twice a second apart, so all six are
          in MFU. Each list runs from its head, the block used last, to its tail, the block that goes first. Press Next
          read to read one block. The block read is outlined, and a block it pushed out has a dashed outline. A clock
          moves one second before each read you make, or 10 ms if you choose.
        </p>
        <p>
          Start with Scan 12 new blocks, then press Next read for each block of the scan. From S11 on the cache is full:
          each new block pushes the oldest scan block out of MRU, and the hot set stays in MFU. Then select S2 on the MRU
          ghost list. It is read from the disk again and goes to MFU, and the MRU target rises from 8 to 8.5 blocks. Read
          a few scan blocks again to move them to MFU, then press Next read until MFU gives up a hot block, and read that
          block back from the MFU ghost list: the MRU target goes down again. Last, choose 10 ms and read the same new
          block twice. The second read comes too soon to count as a second use, so the block stays in MRU.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ArcLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="code">
      <h2 id="code">In the code</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        The ARC is in <code>arc.c</code>. Open a Source note to see the lines, taken from release zfs-2.4.4.
      </p>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Read once, read twice</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A read that finds its block in MRU goes through <code>arc_access</code>. If more than about 62 ms have passed
          since the block’s last counted read, the block moves to MFU. Reads closer together count as one use, so a
          program that reads the same block several times in a burst does not make it look popular.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>arc_access</code>, a hit in MRU, arc.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={A}
            from={5423}
            to={5428}
            symbol="arc_access"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 5423,
                to: 5424,
                text: `<code>b_arc_access</code> is the time of the last counted read. <code>ARC_MINTIME</code> is <a href="${L.mintime}"><code>hz &gt;&gt; 4</code>, “62 ms”</a>. The test is strict: exactly 62 ms does not promote.`,
              },
              {
                from: 5425,
                to: 5427,
                text: `The time is refreshed and the header moves to MFU. Otherwise nothing changes, not even <code>b_arc_access</code>, so a series of re-reads is measured from the first one. A new block enters MRU <a href="${L.anon}">a few lines above</a>, and a hit on an MFU block <a href="${L.mfuHit}">stays in MFU</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Which list gives up a block</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          When the cache is over its target size, <code>arc_c</code>, an eviction pass runs in <code>arc_evict</code>.
          MRU’s target is a fraction of the cache, <code>arc_pd</code>. MRU gives up blocks from its tail only while it is
          over that target, and only as many as the cache is over. MFU gives up the rest. This is how a scan stays in MRU:
          each new scan block puts the cache one block over, and MRU, which is over its target, gives up its own oldest
          block.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>arc_evict</code>, MRU then MFU, arc.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={A}
            from={4543}
            to={4556}
            symbol="arc_evict"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 4545,
                text: `The MRU data target: <code>wt</code>, <a href="${L.wt}">what can be evicted</a>, times <code>arc_pd</code>, a fraction of 2<sup>32</sup>. In the figure <code>wt</code> is <code>arc_c</code>, 16 blocks, and <code>arc_pd</code> starts at one half.`,
              },
              {
                from: 4546,
                to: 4547,
                text: `At most what MRU holds over its target, and no more than the cache is over <code>arc_c</code>. <a href="${L.evictImpl}">A negative amount evicts nothing</a>. In the figure’s first scan MRU keeps 10 blocks against a target of 8, because each new block puts the cache only one block over.`,
              },
              {
                from: 4553,
                to: 4554,
                text: "MFU gives up what is still over. Before these lines the same steps run for metadata, which the figure does not have.",
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Pushed out, but not forgotten</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Evicting a block in <code>arc_evict_hdr</code> frees its data, and its header moves to the matching ghost list:
          MRU ghost for an MRU block, MFU ghost for an MFU block. The header stays in the ARC’s hash table, so a later read
          of the same block finds it.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>arc_evict_hdr</code>, arc.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={A}
            from={3876}
            to={3882}
            symbol="arc_evict_hdr"
            notes={[
              { from: 3876, to: 3880, text: "The data is freed, and the raw copy too if the block has one." },
              {
                from: 3882,
                text: `<code>evicted_state</code> is <a href="${L.evictedState}">chosen just above</a>: MRU ghost for MRU, MFU ghost for MFU. Only the header of an uncached buffer <a href="${L.destroy}">is destroyed here</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>How many ghosts to keep</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          At the end of the same pass, each ghost list is trimmed. A ghost list as large as the whole cache would remember
          reads from too far back, so the code holds each one to half of what the other lists hold. A header trimmed off a
          ghost list is destroyed. Reading that block again is a new block to the ARC, with no ghost hit.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>arc_evict</code>, the ghost lists, arc.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={A}
            from={4561}
            to={4572}
            symbol="arc_evict"
            notes={[
              { from: 4565, to: 4567, text: "The reason for the half: a larger ghost list would give ghost hits from too far back." },
              {
                from: 4569,
                to: 4572,
                text: `The target of the MRU data ghost list, <code>gsrd</code>, is half of the three other live lists: in the figure, which has no metadata, half of MFU. The list is trimmed to it from its tail, and the trimmed headers <a href="${L.ghostDestroy}">are destroyed</a>. <a href="${L.mfuTrim}">The MFU ghost list</a> gets half of MRU in the same way. The value is kept for the next pass.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>A ghost hit</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A read that finds its block’s header on a ghost list is still a miss, and the data is read from the disk.
          <code>arc_access</code> adds the block’s size to that ghost list’s hit counter and puts the block back in the
          cache, in MFU, since this is its second use. It changes no target itself. It only adds up bytes, and the next
          eviction pass reads them.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>arc_access</code>, an MRU ghost hit, arc.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={A}
            from={5432}
            to={5441}
            symbol="arc_access"
            notes={[
              { from: 5432, to: 5435, text: "The comment gives the idea: with a bigger MRU, this read would have been a hit." },
              {
                from: 5437,
                to: 5441,
                text: `Counted on the header, and the block’s size (<a href="${L.hdrSize}">its compressed size if it is compressed</a>) added to the MRU ghost list’s hit counter, which the figure shows under each ghost list. Then <a href="${L.toMfu}">the header goes to MFU</a>, or to MRU if the read was a prefetch. <a href="${L.mfuGhost}">A hit on the MFU ghost list</a> does the same with that list’s counter.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>From ghost hits to a new share</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          At the start of each eviction pass, <code>arc_evict</code> takes the ghost hits counted since the previous pass
          and moves the shares with <code>arc_evict_adj</code>. Hits on the MRU ghost list raise MRU’s share of data,
          <code>arc_pd</code>, and hits on the MFU ghost list lower it. <code>arc_meta</code> and <code>arc_pm</code> do
          the same for metadata, which the figure leaves out.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>arc_evict</code>, the balance, arc.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={A}
            from={4493}
            to={4497}
            symbol="arc_evict"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 4494,
                to: 4495,
                text: `<code>arc_meta</code>, the share of metadata in the cache: up with metadata ghost hits, down with data ghost hits, divided by <code>zfs_arc_meta_balance / 100</code>, <a href="${L.balance}">5 by default</a>.`,
              },
              {
                from: 4496,
                text: `<code>arc_pd</code>, the MRU share of data and the figure’s gauge: up with MRU ghost data hits (<code>grd</code>), down with MFU ghost data hits (<code>gfd</code>), both <a href="${L.hits}">counted since the previous pass</a>. The second argument is the size of the two ghost lists’ targets at the previous pass.`,
              },
              {
                from: 4497,
                text: `<code>arc_pm</code>, the same for metadata. All three are <a href="${L.fractions}">fractions of 2<sup>32</sup></a> and start <a href="${L.init}">at 1/4, 1/2 and 1/2</a>. They replace the <code>arc_p</code> of older releases.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>A small step each time</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>arc_evict_adj</code> moves a share by about the hit bytes divided by the size of the ghost lists, and never
          by more than about 1/32 at once. In the figure one ghost hit of 128 KiB, against ghost lists of 8 blocks, reaches
          that limit: <code>arc_pd</code> goes from 50% to 53.125%, and the MRU target from 8 to 8.5 blocks.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>arc_evict_adj</code>, arc.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={A}
            from={4412}
            to={4422}
            symbol="arc_evict_adj"
            notes={[
              { from: 4412, to: 4416, text: "Two reasons for the limit: no overflow in the arithmetic below, and a cap on how fast the shares move." },
              {
                from: 4418,
                to: 4422,
                text: `Hits of 1/16 of the ghost total or more are scaled down to about 1/32 of it. In the figure a hit of 2<sup>17</sup> bytes against a total of 2<sup>20</sup> is scaled by 4 to 2<sup>15</sup>, which moves <code>arc_pd</code> by 2<sup>27</sup>, 1/32 of its range. <a href="${L.adjRest}">The rest of the function</a> also slows a share as it nears 0 or 1.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>A second cache on fast disks</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A cache device, the L2ARC, can extend the ARC, on solid-state disks for instance. Eviction does not write to it.
          A feed thread copies blocks from the tails of MRU and MFU to the device before they are pushed out, and a ghost
          header whose block is on the device is kept when it is trimmed.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label">The L2ARC design comment, arc.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={A}
            from={8266}
            to={8270}
            symbol="Level 2 ARC"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 8266,
                to: 8268,
                text: `The feed thread, <a href="${L.feed}"><code>l2arc_feed_thread</code></a>, wakes <a href="${L.feedHz}">about once a second</a> and reads the lists <a href="${L.feedTail}">from their tails</a>, the end that goes first. <a href="${L.noEvictPath}">Point 1</a> of the same comment says that eviction writes nothing to the device.`,
              },
              {
                from: 8269,
                to: 8270,
                text: `It copies only what is not already there, and keeps a margin ahead of eviction. A ghost header whose block is on the device <a href="${L.l2cOnly}">stays as L2C only</a>, and a read of it <a href="${L.l2cHit}">goes back to MRU</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>
      <SourcePanel lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figure runs the functions above over data blocks only, all of 128 KiB and uncompressed. The metadata lists are
        empty, so the metadata shares, <code>arc_meta</code> and <code>arc_pm</code>, change nothing and are not drawn.
        Each state is one list. The code spreads headers over several sublists and evicts from them in turn, so its order
        is close to the order of last use but not exactly that order. Here a pass runs right after each read that puts the
        cache over its size. In the code a thread runs it, woken once the cache is over by more than the largest record
        size, 16 MiB on 64-bit systems, which at the figure’s scale would be eight caches. <code>arc_c</code> is fixed,
        while the code grows it as data arrives and shrinks it under memory pressure. The clock is logical, and each block
        is released as soon as it is read. There is no prefetch, no uncached or dirty buffer, no compressed or encrypted
        copy and no L2ARC.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="arc" />
</Base>

